(You will rejoice to hear that no disaster has accompanied the commencement of an) Tj
T*
(enterprise which you have regarded with such evil forebodings. I arrived here yesterday,) Tj
T*
(and my first task is to assure my dear sister of my welfare and increasing confidence in) Tj
T*
(the success of my undertaking.) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
0.043 0.051 0.075 rg
1 0 0 1 64 532.39 Tm
15.500 TL
(I am already far north of London, and as I walk in the streets of Petersburgh, I feel a) Tj
T*
(cold northern breeze play upon my cheeks, which braces my nerves and fills me with) Tj
T*
(delight. Do you understand this feeling? This breeze, which has travelled from the) Tj
T*
(regions towards which I am advancing, gives me a foretaste of those icy climes.) Tj
T*
(Inspirited by this wind of promise, my daydreams become more fervent and vivid. I try in) Tj
T*
(vain to be persuaded that the pole is the seat of frost and desolation; it ever presents) Tj
T*
(itself to my imagination as the region of beauty and delight. There, Margaret, the sun is) Tj
T*
(for ever visible, its broad disk just skirting the horizon and diffusing a perpetual) Tj
T*
(splendour. There--for with your leave, my sister, I will put some trust in preceding) Tj
T*
(navigators--there snow and frost are banished; and, sailing over a calm sea, we may be) Tj
T*
(wafted to a land surpassing in wonders and in beauty every region hitherto discovered on) Tj
T*
(the habitable globe. Its productions and features may be without example, as the) Tj
T*
(phenomena of the heavenly bodies undoubtedly are in those undiscovered solitudes. What) Tj
T*
(may not be expected in a country of eternal light? I may there discover the wondrous) Tj
T*
(power which attracts the needle and may regulate a thousand celestial observations that) Tj
T*
(require only this voyage to render their seeming eccentricities consistent for ever. I) Tj
T*
(shall satiate my ardent curiosity with the sight of a part of the world never before) Tj
T*
(visited, and may tread a land never before imprinted by the foot of man. These are my) Tj
T*
(enticements, and they are sufficient to conquer all fear of danger or death and to induce) Tj
T*
(me to commence this laborious voyage with the joy a child feels when he embarks in a) Tj
T*
(little boat, with his holiday mates, on an expedition of discovery up his native river.) Tj
T*
(But supposing all these conjectures to be false, you cannot contest the inestimable) Tj
T*
(benefit which I shall confer on all mankind, to the last generation, by discovering a) Tj
T*
(passage near the pole to those countries, to reach which at present so many months are) Tj
T*
(requisite; or by ascertaining the secret of the magnet, which, if at all possible, can) Tj
T*
(only be effected by an undertaking such as mine.) Tj
ET
